>
  <h2 id="quick-answer">What is the difference between behavioral science and psychology?</h2>
  <p>
    In my research across both fields, the cleanest distinction I have found is this: psychology is a
    single academic discipline devoted to the scientific study of mind and behavior. Behavioral science
    is an interdisciplinary field that studies human behavior and decision-making by combining
    psychology with economics, sociology, anthropology, and neuroscience. In short: psychology is one
    discipline; behavioral science is a team of disciplines working on the same questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The confusion is understandable because behavioral psychology, a subfield of psychology, sits
    inside behavioral science too. Think of it as nesting: behavioral psychology lives within
    psychology, and psychology contributes to the broader behavioral science umbrella. The rest of
    this article unpacks what each one covers, how they compare, and what the difference means for
    your education and career.
  </p>

  <h2 id="behavioral-science">What does behavioral science cover?</h2>
  <p>
    Behavioral science studies how people make decisions and behave, drawing on several disciplines
    at once. A behavioral scientist studying retirement savings might combine psychological research
    on present bias, economic models of incentives, and sociological data on norms. No single
    discipline owns the question; the field borrows whatever tools explain the behavior.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its signature contributions are applied. Nudge theory, popularized by Richard Thaler and Cass
    Sunstein, shows how small changes in choice architecture, like making organ donation opt-out
    rather than opt-in, shift behavior at scale. Behavioral insights teams in governments apply
    these findings to tax compliance, public health, and energy use. In industry, behavioral
    scientists work in user-experience research, product design, and organizational behavior,
    wherever understanding real human decisions improves outcomes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Methodologically, behavioral science is pluralist. It uses experiments from psychology, surveys
    and field studies from sociology, statistical modeling from economics, and brain imaging from
    neuroscience. The unifying thread is not a method but a problem: explaining and influencing
    human behavior in real-world settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    The applied side has an origin story with a date. In 2010 the UK government created the
    Behavioural Insights Team, often called the "nudge unit," to apply behavioral findings to public
    policy, from tax letters to organ donation defaults. Similar units now exist in governments and
    companies worldwide.
  </p>
  <p>
    One framework behavioral scientists borrow constantly is dual-process theory: Daniel Kahneman's
    distinction between fast, automatic System 1 thinking and slow, deliberate System 2 thinking,
    popularized in his 2011 book Thinking, Fast and Slow. Nudges work by designing for System 1.
  </p>

  <h2 id="psychology">What does psychology cover as a discipline?</h2>
  <p>
    Psychology is the scientific study of mind and behavior as a standalone discipline with its own
    departments, journals, licensing systems, and professional organizations like the American
    Psychological Association. It contains many subfields: clinical, cognitive, developmental,
    social, industrial-organizational, and behavioral psychology among them.
  </p>
  <p>
    What distinguishes psychology is depth within its own traditions. Clinical psychology trains
    practitioners to assess and treat mental health conditions. Cognitive psychology maps memory,
    attention, and reasoning. Developmental psychology traces growth across the lifespan.
    <a href="/what-is-behavioral-psychology/">Behavioral psychology</a> studies how the environment
    shapes observable behavior through learning. Each subfield has its own theories, methods, and
    training paths, but all share the discipline's scientific standards.
  </p>
  <p>
    Psychology also has something behavioral science lacks as a field: a licensure system. Only
    graduates of accredited psychology, counseling, or social work programs can become licensed
    therapists or clinical psychologists. That credentialing power makes psychology the route for
    anyone who wants to practice clinically.
  </p>

  <h2 id="comparison">How do behavioral science and psychology compare?</h2>
  <p>
    The core difference is structure: psychology is one discipline with its own departments,
    journals, and licensing system, while behavioral science is an interdisciplinary field that
    borrows methods from psychology, economics, sociology, and neuroscience. They overlap heavily
    in research, but only psychology offers a clinical licensure path.
  </p>
