  </div>

  <h2 id="degree-paths">Which degree paths fit each field?</h2>
  <p>
    Dedicated behavioral science degrees exist at the bachelor's and master's levels, but many
    practitioners build the profile through a psychology, economics, or public policy degree with
    behavioral coursework. Psychology's path is more standardized: bachelor's, then master's for
    applied roles, then a PhD or PsyD for clinical practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Behavioral science degrees.</strong> Dedicated bachelor's and master's programs in
    behavioral science, behavioral economics, or decision science exist but are less common than
    psychology programs. Many students build the equivalent profile through a psychology,
    economics, or public policy degree with behavioral coursework and research experience. At the
    doctoral level, PhDs in psychology, economics, or business with a behavioral focus are the
    standard research credentials.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Psychology degrees.</strong> The path is more standardized: an associate or bachelor's
    in psychology, then a master's for counseling, school psychology, or applied roles, then a PhD
    or PsyD for clinical practice and senior research roles. Our <a href="/degrees/">degree hub</a>
    walks through each level, including specialized tracks like the
    <a href="/degrees/ms-behavioral-psychology/">MS in behavioral psychology</a> and the
    <a href="/degrees/phd-in-behavioral-psychology/">PhD in behavioral psychology</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two paths converge in research. A behavioral science researcher and a psychology researcher
    may run similar experiments and publish in overlapping journals. They diverge at the applied
    end: only the psychology path leads to clinical licensure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="careers">What careers fit each field?</h2>
  <p>
    Behavioral science careers cluster around applied decision research: UX research, policy
    analysis, and behavioral insights units in government. Psychology careers include those roles
    plus licensed clinical positions like therapist and clinical psychologist. Salaries vary more by
    role, setting, and seniority than by which field name is on the degree.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Behavioral science careers</strong> cluster around applied decision research: UX
    research in tech, behavioral insights units in government, policy analysis, market research,
    and organizational development consulting. These roles value experimental skills, statistics,
    and the ability to translate findings into interventions like nudges and choice architecture.
    Our <a href="/careers/">careers hub</a> covers several of these in detail.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Psychology careers</strong> include everything above plus the licensed clinical roles
    that define the discipline for most people: clinical psychologist, counselor, school
    psychologist, and marriage and family therapist. Research psychologists work in universities,
    healthcare systems, and industry. Industrial-organizational psychologists apply psychological
    science to workplaces, overlapping with the behavioral science side.
  </p>
  <p>
    In my experience, salaries vary more by role, setting, and seniority than by field label.
    Licensed clinicians in private practice and senior industry researchers are among the higher
    earners in both camps; entry-level research positions start modestly in both.
  </p>

  <h2 id="which-to-study">Which should you study: behavioral science or psychology?</h2>
  <p>
    This is educational guidance, not personalized advice, but the decision framework is
    straightforward. Choose psychology if you want the option of clinical licensure, if you want
    depth in one discipline's theories and methods, or if you are drawn to therapy, assessment, or
    traditional research careers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Choose behavioral science, or a psychology degree with a behavioral focus, if you are drawn to
    interdisciplinary problems: why people save too little, how to design better public policies,
    how to build products people actually use. The field suits students who like borrowing tools
    from economics, data science, and design alongside psychology.
  </p>
  <p>
    Either way, the foundations overlap heavily. Undergraduate psychology coursework serves both
    paths, and graduate programs in both fields value research experience, statistics, and clear
    writing. I always tell students the same thing: start with the problems that interest you most,
    and let the degree follow.
  </p>
